{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>בראשית</b> Our sages said that the bet is superfluous like the bet in בָּרִאשֹׁנָה (Genesis 13:4), for we find ״רִאשֹׁנָה יִסָּעוּ״ (Numbers 2:9). But if its sense were so, the bet would be vowelized with a kamatz gadol. Some say that בראשית is always in construct state, and the meaning is 'the beginning of the evening' or 'of the night' or 'of the darkness.' But they forgot ״וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית לוֹ״ (Deuteronomy 33:21). Some say the bet is superfluous without reason, and their explanation is that it should not arise in the heart that there is no beginning to the heavens and the earth, therefore it says בראשית. In my opinion it is in construct state, like ״בְּרֵאשִׁית מַמְלֶכֶת יְהוֹיָקִים״ (Jeremiah 26:1). Do not wonder how it can be in construct before a past verb, for so too ״תְּחִלַּת דְּבַר־יְהוָה בְּהוֹשֵׁעַ וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוָה אֶל־הוֹשֵׁעַ״ (Hosea 1:2), קִרְיַת חַנָּה דָּוִד (Isaiah 29:1). The meaning will be explained to you in the second verse." "<b>ברא</b> Most of the commentators said that creation means bringing forth something from nothing, and so ״אִם־בְּרִיאָה יִבְרָא־יְהֹוָה״ (Numbers 16:30). But they forgot ״וַיִּבְרָא אֱלֹהִים אֶת־הַתַּנִּינִם״ (Genesis 1:21), and three [instances] in one verse, ״וַיִּבְרָא אֱלֹהִים אֶת־הָאָדָם״ (Genesis 1:27), ״וּבוֹרֵא חֹשֶׁךְ״ (Isaiah 45:7) — which is the opposite of light, which is something. This is the grammatical analysis of the word ברא for two reasons: this is the first, and the second, ״לֹא־בָרָאתִי חֹלֶם״ (II Samuel 12:17), which [text] has alef in place of heh, for it is like ״וַיָּבֹא כָל־הָעָם לְהַבְרִיא אֶת־דָּוִד״ (II Samuel 3:35), for it is of the intensive (heavy) stem with added [form]. If it had alef, it would be like לְהַבְרִיאֲכֶם מֵרֵאשִׁית כָּל־מִנְחַת יִשְׂרָאֵל (I Samuel 2:29). And we find from the intensive stem ״וּבְרֵאתָ לְךָ שֵׁם״ (Joshua 17:15), which is not like בְּרוּ לָכֶם אִישׁ (I Samuel 16:8) but like ״וּבָרְאוּ אוֹתָם״ (Ezekiel 23:47), and its meaning is to cut and set a fixed boundary. And the discerning will understand." "Elohim — since we find אֱלוֹהַּ, we know that אֱלֹהִים is a plural form. This root follows linguistic convention, for every language has a mode of honor, and honor in a foreign tongue means the lesser speaks to the greater in plural form. In the language of Ishmael the mode of honor is for the great one, such as a king, to speak in plural form. In the holy language the mode of honor is to use plural form for the great one, like אֲדוֹנִים and בְּעָלִים, of which they said ״אֲדוֹנִים קָשֶׁה״ (Isaiah 19:4), ״וְעַד בְּעָלָיו״ (Exodus 22:14). And so the words עָלָיו, אֵלָיו, עֲדָיו. Therefore Scripture says ברא and not בראו. From wisdom we know that the faculties are called by the name of the organ, because it appears that [speech] emerges from it; and so the supreme soul of man is called lev (heart), and the heart is a body and it is not a body, because the heart is its first vehicle. And because every deed of HaShem is in the hand of the angels who do His will, it is called so. In the verse ״כִּי שְׁמִי בְּקִרְבּוֹ״ (Exodus 23:21) I will explain a little of the sod of the Name. Do not set your mind on the words of the Gaon [Saadia], who said that man is more honored than the angels, for I have already explained in the Book of the Foundation that all his proofs are inverted. We know that there are none more honored among human beings than the prophets, and Joshua fell on his face before the angel of YHWH and bowed, and said, מָה אֲדֹנִי מְדַבֵּר אֶל־עַבְדּוֹ (Joshua 5:14), and so Zechariah and Daniel — why should I prolong? The meaning of Elohei Tzeva'ot is like Elohei ha-Elohim, and the meaning of Elohim is like 'king,' and human beings who engage in the judgment of Elohim are called so. And this name is an attribute, not a substance, and no past or future is found from it. Do not think that the angels are of fire and spirit because we find עֹשֶׂה מַלְאָכָיו רוּחוֹת (Psalms 104:4), for that is not the way of its plain sense. Rather David spoke first about the work of creation, and began with light, and said עֹטֶה אוֹר, and afterward נוֹטֶה שָׁמַיִם, which is the rakia, and the waters upon it, and the fire and snow and wind. And he said that the wind is the messenger of HaShem to go wherever He sends it, and so the fire is among His ministers. And he said יָסַד אֶרֶץ עַל־מְכוֹנֶיהָ, and this is the dry land. And so it is written: רוּחַ סְעָרָה עֹשָׂה דְבָרוֹ (Psalms 148:8)." "The meaning of et is like the essence of the thing, and it is a sign with the verb, like et ha-shamayim. Sometimes it is omitted, like ״אֲשֶׁר־בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אָדָם״ (Deuteronomy 4:32). It is also found with the verb, like וְבָא הָאַרְיֵה וְאֶת־הַדֹּב (I Samuel 17:34), and they are definite in number, and it serves in place of עם (with) and in place of מן (from)." "Ha-shamayim with the definite article, to indicate that it speaks of these that are seen. It is always plural in form, and its grammatical structure may be that of each and every one [i.e., distributed singular]. The meaning of shamayim is height and elevation, and so in the language of Ishmael, most of which follows the pattern of the language of the Greeks [sic]. There is שְׁמֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם, and they are inseparable. Forced [interpretations], and tzerorim, and tzohorayim — and men of the measures [astronomers] will understand these secrets. The Gaon [Saadia] says that the earth is like a point and the heavens like an encompassing line. After these two were created, all that is within them was created, like water and fire. Others say that the waters are included in 'the earth' and the spirit in 'the heavens.' In my opinion, these heavens and earth are the rakia and the dry land, for only one thing was created on each day: on the first, light; on the second, the rakia; on the third, vegetation; on the fourth, the luminaries; on the fifth and sixth, the souls of living creatures. The aforementioned psalm is a witness. It is impossible for the encompassing line to precede the point, or it to precede the line; therefore they said that the heavens and the earth were created at once, and the witness is ״קוֹרֵא אֲנִי אֲלֵיהֶם יַעַמְדוּ יַחְדָּו״ (Isaiah 48:13). This testimony is not clear, because the interpretation of the verse is not so, for how can He say to them when they do not exist, and how can He call what is tohu? Rather its meaning is: I created them, and when I call them they will stand together before Him like servants to do His will, like the meaning of ״דְּבָרְךָ נִצָּב בַּשָּׁמָיִם״ (Psalms 119:89). Afterward He mentioned the earth, and said afterward that they too will stand to do the judgment He commands them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra appears to write נושא (carries) but the correction נוסף (added/superfluous) is indicated by the צ״ל notation; translated according to the corrected reading.", 4 "Reading לשון הקרש as 'language of the Greeks' based on the common medieval Hebrew usage of קרש/גרש for Greek; the word is textually uncertain. The terms כרחים, צהרים (lit. forced, noons/meridians) are rendered as they stand; Ibn Ezra hints at technical astronomical meanings he leaves cryptic."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T12:21:55.370333157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}